<!DOCTYPE html>
<html
  lang={assigns[:locale] || "th"}
  data-theme={SprintLensWeb.Layouts.initial_theme(assigns)}
  data-theme-source={SprintLensWeb.Layouts.theme_source(assigns)}
>
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="csrf-token" content={get_csrf_token()} />
    <.live_title default="SprintLens" suffix=" · SprintLens" phx-no-format>
      {assigns[:page_title]}
    </.live_title>
    <%!--
      Icons and social metadata. There were none of either: the favicon worked
      only because browsers ask for /favicon.ico without being told to, and a
      shared link previewed as nothing at all.

      `og:url` is the site rather than the page on purpose. A retrospective is
      private, and a link preview that named the session somebody just shared
      would be a small leak for no gain.
    --%>
    <link rel="icon" href={~p"/favicon.ico"} sizes="32x32" />
    <link rel="icon" href={~p"/images/favicon.svg"} type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href={~p"/apple-touch-icon.png"} />
    <link rel="manifest" href={~p"/site.webmanifest"} />
    <%!--
      Two of them, keyed on the colour scheme: one value would leave the
      browser's own chrome clashing with whichever theme it did not match.
      These are the two surfaces, not the brand, so the chrome disappears into
      the page.
    --%>
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#f8f8f8" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#292f37" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
    <meta name="description" content={gettext("Retrospectives your team actually acts on")} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="SprintLens" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="SprintLens" />
    <meta
      property="og:description"
      content={gettext("Retrospectives your team actually acts on")}
    />
    <meta property="og:url" content={SprintLensWeb.Endpoint.url()} />
    <meta
      property="og:image"
      content={SprintLensWeb.Endpoint.url() <> ~p"/images/og-image.png"}
    />
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <link phx-track-static rel="stylesheet" href={~p"/assets/css/app.css"} />
    <script defer phx-track-static type="text/javascript" src={~p"/assets/js/app.js"}>
    </script>
    <%!--
      Runs before first paint so the page never flashes the wrong theme
      (FR-911). The server has already stamped data-theme from the profile or
      the session, so this is left with one job: resolving "system" against
      the operating system preference, which is the one thing the server
      cannot know, and keeping it live when that preference changes.

      There is deliberately no localStorage here. The theme is chosen through
      a link that a controller handles, so the server is the single place it
      lives; a second copy in the browser could only disagree with it.
    --%>
    <script>
      (() => {
        const root = document.documentElement;
        const systemTheme = () => matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches ? "dark" : "light";

        const followSystem = () => {
          if (root.getAttribute("data-theme-source") === "system") {
            root.setAttribute("data-theme", systemTheme());
          }
        };

        followSystem();

        matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").addEventListener("change", followSystem);
      })();
    </script>
  </head>
  <body class="min-h-screen bg-base-100 text-base-content">
    {@inner_content}
  </body>
</html>
